{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c59f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1aa23-73f9-7152-a6b5-3fcdf5d3c2f1"},"sequence":298,"recorded_at":1780531400000591,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01a1aa23-76a1-7912-8c1f-0bbd20af817a","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"command_id":"01a1aa23-76a1-7912-8c1f-0bbd20af817a","event":{"chunk":"Read text file `Fit Getaway-Wii Fit-muse-wonder-1.0-max-20261104-221652/GUIDE.md`.\n1|# Pixel Engine: the guide\n2|\n3|> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. The kit: `README.md` (what is where), this guide, `API.md`, `pixel-engine.js`.\n4|\n5|This guide is for an AI agent (or a person) building a game on Pixel Engine from the kit:\n6|`pixel-engine.js` plus this guide, `API.md` and a starter game. Read it whole once; it is\n7|written to be enough. `API.md` has every export with its exact signature: search it when you\n8|need a name or a parameter, don't read it whole.\n9|\n10|Contents: 1 Host it · 2 Mental model · 3 Quick start · 4 The API by system · 5 Recipes ·\n11|6 Rules · 7 Common mistakes · 8 Debugging and checking · 9 Before you hand back · 10 Versions\n12|\n13|## 1. Host it\n14|\n15|A game is static files next to the engine. Nothing to install, no build step:\n16|\n17|```\n18|my-game/\n19|  pixel-engine.js     the engine (never edit it; upgrade by replacing it)\n20|  index.html          a page with <div id=\"app\"> that loads game.js as a module\n21|  game.js             your game: import { ... } from './pixel-engine.js'\n22|  assets/             your own .glb models, if any (optional)\n23|```\n24|\n25|The kit's `index.html` and `game.js` are exactly that: copy the kit folder and edit `game.js`.\n26|Split a bigger game into more modules (`import { Level } from './level.js'`) as usual.\n27|\n28|- **Serve it over HTTP.** ES modules don't load from `file://`. Any static server works:\n29|  `npx serve .`, `python3 -m http.server`, GitHub Pages, Netlify, an S3 bucket.\n30|- **One import.** `pixel-engine.js` is one self-contained ES module (about 4.8 MB, 1.6 MB\n31|  gzipped). It holds the engine, three.js r186 (as `THREE` and `TSL`), Rapier physics with its\n32|  wasm, the hero (model, rig and every animation clip), three built-in models (`assets/hero.glb`,\n33|  `assets/coin.glb`, `assets/tree.glb`) and the engine's CSS. Don't load anything else.\n34|- **The page is the game's.** Importing the engine adds its CSS, which makes the page a\n35|  full-screen game: `html, body` don't scroll and `#app` fills the window. To embed a game in a\n36|  bigger page, give the container your own size and position (your CSS comes later and wins).\n37|- **Browsers.** WebGPU only: a recent Chrome or Edge, Safari 26, Firefox 141 on Windows. A\n38|  browser without it is told so (\"Failed to start: WebGPU unavailable: …\"). Phones and tablets\n39|  get on-screen touch controls by themselves.\n40|- **Your own models.** Put GLBs in `assets/` and call `ctx.loadModel('assets/ship.glb')`\n41|  (paths are relative to the page). To ship one file with no `assets/` folder, set\n42|  `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__ = { 'assets/ship.glb': 'data:model/gltf-binary;base64,...' }`\n43|  in a classic `<script>` before the module loads.\n44|\n45|## 2. Mental model\n46|\n47|**The engine renders 3D as pixel art.** You build a small 3D world out of chunky low-poly\n48|shapes and toon materials in a 16-colour palette. The renderer draws it at a low art\n49|resolution (480×270, wider or taller to fit the screen), adds pixel outlines and scales it\n50|up by whole pixels. You never draw sprites; the pixel art comes from the camera and the\n51|pipeline. A **look** can change that: other palettes, CRT, cel shading, or clean\n52|full-resolution rendering, per layer (section 4, *Looks*).\n53|\n54|**The engine owns the machinery; a game is a plain object with hooks.** The engine owns the\n55|renderer, camera rig, physics stepping, the sun and ambient light, input, audio, particles,\n56|the pixel HUD, the loading screen and the debug tools. You write a class:\n57|\n58|| Hook | When | Do here |\n59|| --- | --- | --- |\n60|| `name`, `assets` | before start | the title; models to start downloading early |\n61|| `async setup(ctx)` | once | build the level: meshes, colliders, models, characters, triggers |\n62|| `fixedUpdate(ctx, dt)` | 60 times a second, before each physics step | movement, forces, anything gameplay that must be exact |\n63|| `update(ctx, dt)` | every rendered frame, after physics | animation, visuals, pickups, HUD |\n64|| `cameraTarget(ctx)` | every frame | return the `Vector3` the camera follows |\n65|| `status(ctx)` | tooling | a one-line summary (`'coins 3/4'`), shown by the check tool |\n66|| `fingerprint(ctx)` | tooling | the game's own state as a string (positions, counters), part of `engine.fingerprint()` so a replay can be compared |\n67|| `snapshot(ctx)`, `restore(ctx, state)`, `beforeRestore(ctx)` | save-states | the game's own state as plain data (as it is, not through JSON), and back (`await engine.snapshot()`, `await engine.restore(snap)`); physics bodies are the engine's: keep their handles; `beforeRestore` leaves a mode with its own camera first |\n68|| `onCameraChange(ctx)`, `eyePosition(ctx)`, `dispose(ctx)` | optional | camera swaps, first-person eye, cleanup |\n69|| `ready(ctx)` | optional: when the engine starts running the game, and after each `loadGame` | page handles for tools (`window.__MYGAME__`) |\n70|\n71|`ctx` (the `GameContext`) is the same object in every hook: `ctx.scene` (three.js scene),\n72|`ctx.physics`, `ctx.input`, `ctx.camera`, `ctx.audio`, `ctx.particles`, `ctx.hud`,\n73|`ctx.lights`, `ctx.palette`, `ctx.loadModel`, `ctx.time`, `ctx.random` and `ctx.engine`.\n74|\n75|**Physics owns positions; visuals follow.** Bodies move in fixed 60 Hz steps (Rapier).\n76|Meshes are interpolated between steps, so motion is smooth at any frame rate. A character\n77|controller moves its body in `fixedUpdate`; its model is posed in `update`.\n78|\n79|**Scale.** One unit is one metre, Y is up, the ground is usually at y = 0. The hero is 1.6 m\n80|tall, runs at 6.5 m/s and jumps 1.7 m (double jump 2.4 m, triple 3.5 m; it also grabs ledges).\n81|At the default zoom the camera shows about 13.5 m vertically, so one art pixel is about\n82|5 cm: give shapes at least 0.25 m so they read.\n83|\n84|## 3. Quick start\n85|\n86|A complete game, and the kit's `game.js`. Walk with WASD or the arrows, jump with Space,\n87|collect the four coins.\n88|\n89|```js quickstart\n90|// Coin Garden: walk (WASD / arrows / stick), jump (Space), collect every coin.\n91|import { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n92|\n93|const { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n94|\n95|/** A box you can see (toon material, palette colour) and stand on (a static collider). */\n96|function block(ctx, [x, y, z], [w, h, d], color) {\n97|  ctx.physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\n98|  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\n99|  mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n100|  mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\n101|  return mesh;\n102|}\n103|\n104|class CoinGarden {\n105|  name = 'Coin Garden';\n106|  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb']; // start downloading before setup runs\n107|  coins = [];\n108|  collected = 0;\n109|  won = false;\n110|  target = new Vector3();\n111|  ground = new Vector3();\n112|\n113|  async setup(ctx) {\n114|    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n115|    // Level: static geometry merged into one draw call per material; one collider per block.\n116|    scene.add(\n117|      ...mergeStaticMeshes([\n118|        block(ctx, [0, -0.5, 0], [24, 1, 24], palette.green),\n119|        block(ctx, [4, 0.5, -2], [3, 1, 3], palette.slate),\n120|        block(ctx, [7, 1.25, -2], [3, 2.5, 3], palette.teal),\n121|        block(ctx, [-5, 0.75, 4], [2, 1.5, 2], palette.orange),\n122|      ]),\n123|    );\n124|    // Coins: a model, spinning; a trigger volume collects it when the hero walks in.\n125|    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb', { castShadow: false });\n126|    for (const [x, y, z] of [[4, 1.4, -2], [7, 2.9, -2], [-5, 1.9, 4], [-2, 0.4, -5]]) {\n127|      const mesh = coin.scene.clone(true);\n128|      mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n129|      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors'); // characters & objects can get their own look\n130|      scene.add(mesh);\n131|      const item = { mesh, taken: false };\n132|      physics.trigger({ cylinder: { halfHeight: 0.6, radius: 0.5 } }, [x, y, z], { tag: 'character', once: true, onEnter: () => this.collect(ctx, item) });\n133|      this.coins.push(item);\n134|    }\n135|    // The hero: the bundled model and clips on a Mario-64-style controller.\n136|    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false });\n137|    this.model = hero.scene;\n138|    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n139|    this.shadow = new ContactShadow(0.42);\n140|    scene.add(this.model, this.shadow);\n141|    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 4], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n142|    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n143|  }\n144|\n145|  collect(ctx, item) {\n146|    item.taken = true;\n147|    item.mesh.visible = false;\n148|    this.collected++;\n149|    ctx.audio.play('coin');\n150|    ctx.particles.burst('sparkle', item.mesh.position);\n151|  }\n152|\n153|  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n154|    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input); // filled in place after the first step\n155|    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input); // movement and forces: fixed 60 Hz step\n156|  }\n157|\n158|  update(ctx, dt) {\n159|    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha); // pose and interpolate\n160|    const feet = this.model.position;\n161|    const ground = ctx.physics.groundBelow(this.ground.set(feet.x, feet.y + 0.1, feet.z), 20, this.hero.body);\n162|    if (ground) this.shadow.place(feet.x, ground.y, feet.z, feet.y - ground.y);\n163|    for (const c of this.coins) if (!c.taken) c.mesh.rotation.y += dt * 3;\n164|    if (!this.won && this.collected === this.coins.length) {\n165|      this.won = true;\n166|      this.hero.celebrate();\n167|      ctx.audio.play('fanfare');\n168|    }\n169|    ctx.hud.clear();\n170|    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `COINS ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`, { color: 'sand' });\n171|    if (this.won) ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'YOU WIN!', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2, shadow: 'navy' });\n172|  }\n173|\n174|  cameraTarget() {\n175|    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 4);\n176|    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n177|  }\n178|\n179|  onCameraChange(ctx) {\n180|    this.hero.setLockDepth(ctx.camera.lockDepth); // side view: stay on the lane\n181|    this.model.visible = !ctx.camera.hidesTarget; // first person: no body in the way\n182|  }\n183|\n184|  status() {\n185|    return `coins ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`;\n186|  }\n187|}\n188|\n189|/** A fresh game: the page starts one, and `node check.mjs --headless` plays one in Node (section 8). */\n190|export const createGame = () => new CoinGarden();\n191|\n192|if (typeof document !== 'undefined') await Engine.start(createGame(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'iso' } }));\n193|```\n194|\n195|To make it yours: change the blocks (the level), the coin spots, the camera preset, the HUD,\n196|the win condition. `withUrlOptions(options)` adds the review flags from the page URL on top of\n197|your options (`?camera=side`, `?zoom=1.5`, `?look=noir`, `?debug=1`; section 8): the camera is\n198|merged key by key, and with no flags your options are used as they are.\n199|\n200|## 4. The API by system\n201|\n202|Everything below comes from `./pixel-engine.js`. `API.md` has the full signatures.\n203|\n204|### Starting\n205|\n206|```js\n207|const engine = await Engine.start(new MyGame(), withUrlOptions({  // + review flags from the URL\n208|  container: document.getElementById('app'),\n209|  camera: { preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.2 },  // one preset per game (see Camera)\n210|  look: LOOK_PRESETS.pixel_heroes,       // optional: the art style (see Looks)\n211|  background: PALETTE.sky,               // clear colour (default PALETTE.night)\n212|}));\n213|await engine.loadGame(new Level2());     // next level: same renderer, camera, input, audio\n214|```\n215|\n216|Shipping options: the debug panel and the engine hotkeys (P, R, \\`, [, ], M) are off outside\n217|`?debug=1`. Players get those options in the settings panel instead (look, pixel art, pixel size,\n218|quality, frame rate, sound, debug info; saved per game): the ⚙ button opens it, top right\n219|(`settings: { button: 'top-left' }` moves it; `false` hides it, for a game that opens it from its\n220|own menu with `engine.settings.open()`). A game that uses R, P or M itself moves that hotkey for\n221|`?debug=1` with `debugKeys: { resolution: 'F2' }`. `touchButtons: [{ label: 'A', code: 'Space', hint: 'jump' }]`\n222|replaces the on-screen buttons.\n223|`loadGame` can be called from anywhere (a trigger, `update`); the swap happens after the\n224|frame. It unloads the old game: scene objects, physics, triggers, particles, HUD, music and\n225|lights are freed, so a level never leaks into the next.\n226|\n227|### Building a level\n228|\n229|- **Materials:** `toonMaterial(color)` is a cached 3-band toon material. Use palette\n230|  colours: `ctx.palette.green` or `PALETTE.green`. The 16 names: `ink plum red orange sand\n231|  lime green teal navy blue sky cyan white mist slate night`. Options: `{ map, vertexColors, rim, dissolve }`.\n232|- **Meshes:** plain three.js (`new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(c))`).\n233|  Set `castShadow` / `receiveShadow`. For the static level, build meshes and add\n234|  `...mergeStaticMeshes(meshes)`: one draw call per material instead of one per block (don't\n235|  add the sources too). Keep anything that moves separate.\n236|- **Colliders:** `physics.addStaticBox({ position, halfExtents, rotationY?, friction? })` and\n237|  `physics.addStaticCylinder(position, halfHeight, radius)`. One per solid block, matching\n238|  the mesh (half extents are half the size). Anything else through `RAPIER` and\n239|  `physics.world` (see the top-down recipe's crates).\n240|- **Collider tags** the hero understands: `physics.tag(collider, 'climbable')`, `'pushable'`,\n241|  `'grabbable'`, `'slippery'`, `'noLedge'`, `'noCamera'` (camera rays ignore it).\n242|- **Models:** `const m = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb')` returns `{ scene, animations }`,\n243|  a fresh clone each call, materials converted to toon. `m.scene.clone(true)` copies a static model.\n244|  List models in the game's `assets` so they download while the engine starts.\n245|- **Look layers:** `setLookLayer(object, 'actors')` puts characters and objects on the actors\n246|  layer (children follow); everything else is environment. Looks can treat them differently.\n247|- **Shadow blob:** `new ContactShadow(radius)`, placed every frame with\n248|  `shadow.place(x, groundY, z, heightAboveGround)` (see the quick start).\n249|\n250|**Models and textures as data.** Besides GLBs, the kit has a library of models (crates, stalls,\n251|lamps, trees, doors, weapons...) and pixel-art textures, and a format to write your own as plain\n252|data: `buildModel('market-stall', { palette: { cloth: 'blue' } })` gives merged toon meshes, their\n253|collider data and light, and `textureMap('cobbles')` a map for `toonMaterial`. `ASSETS.md` is their\n254|guide (the format, the loop, the style rules, an example scene), `LIBRARY.md` lists the library,\n255|and `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws what you write. Read `ASSETS.md` before you write one.\n256|\n257|### The hero\n258|\n259|`PlatformerCharacter` is a Mario-64-style controller with the full moveset and animation;\n260|`HERO_MODEL`, `HERO_RIG` and `HERO_CLIPS` are its model and clips:\n261|\n262|```js\n263|const model = (await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false })).scene;\n264|setLookLayer(model, 'actors');\n265|ctx.scene.add(model);\n266|const hero = new PlatformerCharacter(ctx.physics, { position: [0, 0, 0], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n267|hero.attachModel(model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, model), HERO_RIG);\n268|// fixedUpdate: input = readMoveInput(ctx, hero, input); hero.fixedUpdate(dt, input);  (input kept, filled in place)\n269|// update:      hero.updateVisual(model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n270|```\n271|\n272|Controls (`readMoveInput`, camera-relative in every preset): WASD / arrows move (Shift\n273|walks), Space jumps (again on landing: double, triple; wall kicks), C crouches (+ Space\n274|backflip; running: slide, long jump; in the air: ground pound), J punch / kick / dive, F grab\n275|and pull, Z prone, X lie down, V wave, B sit. Ledges, climbing (`climbable`), pushing\n276|(`pushable`), slopes and stairs just work. Gamepads and touch map onto the same keys.\n277|\n278|The game drives it with: `hero.teleport([x, y, z])` (feet position; resets momentum),\n279|`hero.hurt(from)` (knocked back away from `from`, the direction from the hero to what hit it:\n280|enemy position minus hero position; false while `hero.invulnerable > 0`),\n281|`hero.jump('Jump')` (bounce, e.g. off an enemy), `hero.celebrate()`, `hero.lookAt = vector`\n282|(turns the head), `hero.setLockDepth(on)`. It reports `hero.state` (`'idle'`, `'run'`,\n283|`'jump'`, ...), `hero.grounded`, `hero.vy`, `hero.speed`, `hero.facing`, `hero.stats`\n284|(jumps, landings, hurts, ...), `hero.feetInto(v)` and `hero.body` (its Rapier body). Its\n285|collider carries the `'character'` tag. For a simpler walk-and-jump capsule there is\n286|`CharacterController`.\n287|\n288|### Input\n289|\n290|`ctx.input.isDown('KeyE')` (held), `wasPressed('KeyE')` (once, in `update`),\n291|`consumePress('KeyE')` (once, in `fixedUpdate`: it keeps a press for 150 ms so none is lost\n292|between steps), `moveAxis()` (`{ x, y }` from keys, stick or touch). Codes are\n293|`KeyboardEvent.code` values: `KeyA`, `Space`, `ArrowUp`, `Digit1`, `ShiftLeft`.\n294|\n295|### Physics queries and triggers\n296|\n297|```js\n298|const t = ctx.physics.trigger({ box: [1, 1.5, 0.5] }, [x, y, z], {\n299|  tag: 'character', once: true,                // only the hero; remove itself after the first enter\n300|  onEnter: () => win(), onExit: () => {},\n301|});                                            // shapes: box (half extents), sphere, capsule, cylinder\n302|t.position.set(4, 1, 0); t.enabled = false; t.remove();\n303|ctx.physics.groundBelow(origin, 20, hero.body); // { y, distance } | null\n304|ctx.physics.castRay(from, dir, maxDistance);    // { distance, collider, normal, point } | null\n305|ctx.physics.remove(colliderOrBody);             // e.g. open a gate\n306|```\n307|\n308|Triggers see dynamic and kinematic bodies (the hero, crates), not level geometry. Dynamic\n309|bodies: `RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()` and `RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(...)` on\n310|`ctx.physics.world`, then `ctx.physics.bind(body, mesh)` so the mesh follows it.\n311|\n312|### Physics toys: platforms, fields, joints, destruction, cloth\n313|\n314|```js\n315|const lift = ctx.physics.world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.kinematicPositionBased());\n316|ctx.physics.world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(1.2, 0.15, 1.2), lift);\n317|ctx.physics.addMover(lift, { path: [[0, 0.15, 0], [0, 4, 0]], speed: 1.5, hold: 1 }); // the hero rides it\n318|ctx.physics.addMover(disc, { spin: [0, 0.6, 0] });           // also: swing, curve: orbit(...) / pendulum(...)\n319|ctx.physics.conveyor(beltCollider, [2, 0, 0]);               // carries crates and the hero\n320|ctx.physics.fields.add({ box: [1, 4, 1], at: [4, 4, 0], force: [0, 90, 0], falloff: true, drag: 0.5 }); // an updraft\n321|ctx.physics.explode([0, 0.5, 0], { radius: 4, impulse: 12 });\n322|hero.launch(16);                                             // a trampoline\n323|hero.shove = 1.5; hero.weight = 1;                           // push loose bodies; press dynamic floors (both 0 by default)\n324|ropeBridge(ctx, [-3, 0, 0], [3, 0, 0], { planks: 10 });      // also chain, hingeDoor, springPad, seesaw\n325|const breaks = new Breakables(ctx);                          // breaks.add / break / crumble; update(dt, hero.groundCollider)\n326|const flag = clothGrid({ width: 2, height: 1.2, cols: 12, rows: 8, origin: [0, 3, 0], pin: 'left' });\n327|ctx.scene.add(new SoftMesh(flag, toonMaterial(PALETTE.red)));  // flag.step(dt) per fixed step, mesh.sync() per frame\n328|const balls = new InstancedBodies(ctx.physics, ctx.scene, { shape: 'ball', size: 0.2, capacity: 500 }); // one draw call\n329|```\n330|\n331|Movers are functions of their own clock (same at any frame rate). `hero.stats.crushes` counts a\n332|platform coming down on the hero: decide what it does. Ledges are fixed bodies only.\n333|\n334|### Water, weather, grass, trails and decals\n335|\n336|```js\n337|const water = new WaterSurface({ size: [14, 8], at: [0, -0.2, 0], waves: WAVES_CALM, ripples: new RippleField({ size: [14, 8], cells: [112, 64] }) });\n338|const floaters = new Floaters(ctx.physics.world, (x, z) => (water.covers(x, z) ? water.heightAt(x, z) : null));\n339|applySky(ctx.engine, skyAt(19));                              // time of day: sun, colours, background\n340|const rain = new Precipitation({ kind: 'rain', count: 3000 }); // also 'snow'; rain.intensity, rain.wind\n341|const grass = new GrassField({ area: [20, 14], at: [0, 0, 0], count: 6000 });  // grass.push(0, heroFeet, 0.8)\n342|const decals = new Decals(ctx.scene);                          // decals.add('scorch', at, normal, { size: 2 })\n343|ctx.scene.add(water, rain, grass, new Trail({ width: 0.3 }));  // trail.push(p, t); trail.update(t, camera)\n344|```\n345|\n346|Per frame: `water.update(t)`, `rain.update(dt, focus)`, `grass.update(t)`, `decals.update(dt)`;\n347|per fixed step: `floaters.step(dt)` and the ripple field's `step(dt)`.\n348|\n349|### Procedural motion and sprites\n350|\n351|```js\n352|const tail = new SpringChain({ segments: 6, length: 0.12 });   // tail.update(dt, root, [right, up, forward]); tail.points\n353|const squash = new Squash();                                   // squash.kick(-5); squash.update(dt); squash.scale() → [xz, y]\n354|const legs = new LegStepper({ legs: [{ rest: [-0.6, 0.5], partners: [1] }, { rest: [0.6, 0.5], partners: [0] }] });\n355|legs.update(dt, body, yaw, velocity, (x, z) => 0);             // legs.feet[i].at; knee = twoBoneIK(hip, foot, a, b, pole)\n356|const sheet = drawSheet({ frame: [16, 16], frames: 4 }, (g, f) => { g.fillStyle = '#ffcd75'; g.fillRect(4, 4 + (f % 2), 8, 8); });\n357|const sprites = new SpriteBatch(sheet, { capacity: 100 });     // ctx.scene.add(sprites); sprites.spawn(at, { size: 1 })\n358|```\n359|\n360|Sprites face the camera, keep square pixels and are one draw per sheet: `sprites.set(i, { at, frame, flip })`.\n361|\n362|A ragdoll from the hero model: `const doll = new Ragdoll(ctx.physics, model, HERO_RAGDOLL); doll.enable({ velocity })`,\n363|then `doll.sync()` every frame instead of the mixer; `doll.release({ ground: 0 })` hands it back (blend into `GetUp`).\n364|\n365|### Guards, paths, flocks, cars and bullets\n366|\n367|```js\n368|const nav = NavGrid.fromRows(['#######', '#..c..#', '#.....#', '#######'], { blocked: '#c' });\n369|const path = nav.path([-2, 0, -0.5], [2, 0, 0.5]);         // A* (corners only); nav.lineOfSight(ax, az, bx, bz)\n370|const flock = new Boids(80, { bounds: { min: [-8, 2, -8], max: [8, 5, 8] } }); // flock.step(dt); flock.pos\n371|const car = new Vehicle(ctx.physics, { at: [0, 1, 0] });   // car.drive({ throttle, steer, handbrake }, dt) per fixed step\n372|const pool = new BulletPool(3000);                         // emitter({ pattern: 'spiral', every: 0.06 }).update(pool, dt, at, aim)\n373|```\n374|\n375|### Mouse picking and rewind\n376|\n377|```js\n378|ctx.camera.rayAt(ctx.engine.input.pointer.x, ctx.engine.input.pointer.y, origin, dir); // then ctx.physics.castRay(origin, dir, 1000)\n379|const rewind = new Rewind(ctx.physics, { seconds: 5 }); // rewind.trackAll(); per step: rewind.rewinding = input.isDown('KeyR')\n380|```\n381|\n382|### Procedural generation\n383|\n384|```js\n385|const noise = createNoise(7); // noise.fbm2(x * 0.05, z * 0.05) about -1..1; noise.ridged2 for crests\n386|const land = new Terrain({ size: [40, 30] }).generate((x, z) => 4 * noise.fbm2(x * 0.05, z * 0.05)); // land.erode(); land.attach(ctx.physics); ctx.scene.add(land.mesh)\n387|const wfc = new Wfc(patternTiles([{ pattern: ['#.#', '#.#', '#.#'] }, { pattern: ['###', '###', '###'] }]), 10, 8, { seed: 3 }); // wfc.run(); wfc.result\n388|const plant = turtle(expand(PLANTS.fern.system, 5), PLANTS.fern.turtle); // plant.branches: { from, to, radius }, plant.leaves\n389|```\n390|\n391|### GPU compute, monitors and mirrors\n392|\n393|```js\n394|const swarm = new GpuSwarm({ count: 32768 }); ctx.scene.add(swarm.mesh); // per frame: swarm.update(ctx.engine.renderer, dt)\n395|const cam = new RenderView({ size: [160, 90] }); const stopCam = cam.attach(ctx.engine.renderer, ctx.scene); // screen.material = cam.screenMaterial(); RenderView.screen(screen)\n396|const mirror = new Mirror({ size: [6, 3] }); ctx.scene.add(mirror.mesh); const stopMirror = mirror.attach(ctx.engine.renderer, ctx.scene);\n397|// in dispose(): stopCam(); stopMirror(); cam.dispose(); mirror.dispose(); swarm.dispose() (a level unload also drops the listeners)\n398|const holo = hologramMaterial({ color: PALETTE.cyan }); // forceFieldMaterial, lavaMaterial, marbleMaterial, woodMaterial, crystalMaterial\n399|```\n400|\n401|### Behaviour trees, cutscenes, sound in space\n402|\n403|```js\n404|const tree = new BehaviorTree(bt.selector('npc', bt.sequence('flee', bt.condition('near?', (b) => b.near), bt.action('run', run)), bt.action('wander', wander)), board); // tree.tick(dt) per step\n405|const scene = new Timeline({ shots: [{ at: 0, position: [0, 6, 10], target: [0, 1, 0] }, { at: 4, position: [-1, 2, 4], target: [0, 1, 0] }], cues: [{ at: 1, run: openDoor }], lines: [{ at: 0.5, until: 3, text: 'HELLO.' }] }); // scene.play(); scene.update(dt); scene.camera(); scene.skip()\n406|const hum = ctx.audio.loop('coin', { volume: 0 }); // per frame: const s = spatialize({ position: ears, right }, at, { max: 20 }); hum.set({ volume: s.gain, pan: s.pan, muffle: blocked ? 0.85 : 0 })\n407|```\n408|\n409|### Camera\n410|\n411|| Preset | View | Notes |\n412|| --- | --- | --- |\n413|| `iso` | orthographic 3/4 view, 32° pitch, 45° yaw | the default; `pitch`, `yaw` configurable |\n414|| `topdown` | straight down, screen up = −Z | |\n415|| `side` | straight side-on, screen right = +X | `ctx.camera.lockDepth` is true: pass it to the hero |\n416|| `third` | perspective orbit behind the player | drag or Q/E to orbit |\n417|| `first` | first person, mouse look (click locks the pointer) | `ctx.camera.hidesTarget`: hide your model |\n418|| `free`, `fixed` | authoring fly-cam; a frozen view | `{ preset: 'fixed', position, target, fov }` |\n419|\n420|Config: `{ preset, zoom, minZoom, maxZoom, viewHeight, pitch, yaw, fov, distance, stiffness }`.\n421|Players zoom with the wheel or + / -. `engine.setCamera(config, { syncUrl: false })` swaps\n422|the preset during play (a cutscene, a minigame); the game's `onCameraChange` fixes up\n423|anything preset-specific.\n424|\n425|### HUD\n426|\n427|A pixel canvas over the game, in art pixels, drawn after the filters (always crisp).\n428|It is retained: `clear()` then redraw each frame.\n429|\n430|```js\n431|ctx.hud.clear();\n432|ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `SCORE ${score}`, { color: 'sand' });            // 5×7 font, UPPERCASE\n433|ctx.hud.text(6, 6, 'LIVES 3', { anchor: 'top-right' });              // anchors: top-left (default), top,\n434|ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });        //   top-right, left, center, right, bottom-*\n435|ctx.hud.rect(0, 0, ctx.hud.width, 12, 'ink', 'bottom-left');\n436|ctx.hud.sprite(6, 20, ['.oo.', 'oyyo', '.oo.'], { o: 'orange', y: 'sand' }); // '.' is transparent\n437|```\n438|\n439|### Audio\n440|\n441|```js\n442|ctx.audio.play('coin');                       // built-in: jump doubleJump land coin step skid punch groundPound whoosh hurt fanfare\n443|ctx.audio.play('coin', { pitch: 5, volume: 0.6, pan: -0.3 });\n444|ctx.audio.register('zap', { wave: 'square', duty: 0.25, freq: 1200, freqEnd: 200, decay: 0.15, volume: 0.3 });\n445|ctx.audio.playMusic(SONG);                    // a Song (data, see the top-down recipe); playMusic(null) stops\n446|await ctx.audio.load('door', 'assets/door.ogg');\n447|```\n448|\n449|Sounds are synthesised from data, so a game needs no audio files. Browsers start sound on\n450|the first key or tap; before that, calls are silent (and still counted in `ctx.audio.counts`).\n451|\n452|### Particles and lights\n453|\n454|```js\n455|ctx.particles.burst('dust', at);              // built-in: dust skid sparkle smoke impact\n456|ctx.particles.burst('sparkle', at, { count: 20, colors: ['white', 'cyan'] });\n457|ctx.particles.register('ember', { count: [3, 5], life: [0.6, 1], speed: [1, 2], gravity: -2, size: [1, 1], colors: ['sand', 'orange', 'red'] });\n458|\n459|const torch = ctx.lights.request({ position: [3, 1.6, 4], color: 0xffa040, intensity: 6, radius: 7, flicker: 'torch' });\n460|ctx.lights.request({ follow: heroModel, offset: [0, 1.4, 0], intensity: 5, radius: 6, priority: 8 });\n461|torch.release();\n462|ctx.engine.sun.intensity = 0.6;               // the sun and ambient light are the engine's: dim them for night\n463|```\n464|\n465|Particle sizes are whole art pixels and colours are palette names. Point lights come from a\n466|fixed pool (4 to 16 by quality), handed to the most important requests: never add your own\n467|`PointLight`s (each one recompiles every material).\n468|\n469|### Game feel and scene changes\n470|\n471|```js\n472|ctx.engine.hitstop(0.08);                    // freeze the game 0.08 s when a hit lands (rendering goes on)\n473|ctx.engine.shake.add(0.4);                   // screen shake: trauma 0..1 that drains; whole art pixels\n474|ctx.engine.timeScale = 0.25;                 // slow motion (1 normal, 0 a frozen menu); loadGame resets it\n475|ctx.engine.screen.flash('white', { duration: 0.1 });\n476|ctx.engine.screen.shockwave(blastAt, { radius: 0.4 });        // a ring that pushes the picture outward\n477|await ctx.engine.screen.cover('iris', { center: heroAt });    // fade iris diamonds dissolve dither blinds wipe curtain mosaic\n478|await ctx.engine.loadGame(new Level2());\n479|await ctx.engine.screen.reveal('iris');\n480|ctx.tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' }); // game time, cleared on unload\n481|ctx.tweens.call(1.5, () => spawnWave());     // a timer\n482|ctx.engine.setSunDirection([1, 0.3, 0.2]);   // a low dawn sun; the next level resets it\n483|```\n484|\n485|Transitions, flashes and shockwaves are uniforms at the end of the pipeline: they never\n486|compile a shader, work with every look, and keep running while a level loads.\n487|\n488|### Looks\n489|\n490|A `Look` sets the art style per layer: `{ scene, actors, environment }`. Each layer is\n491|`{ pixel: { size, outline, crease } | null, filters: [...] }` (`null` renders it clean, at\n492|full resolution); `scene` filters apply to the whole frame on top.\n493|\n494|```js\n495|engine.setLook(LOOK_PRESETS.noir);            // or Engine.start(game, { look }) / ?look=noir\n496|engine.setLook({\n497|  scene: [{ id: 'vignette' }],\n498|  actors: { pixel: { size: 2, outline: 0.8, crease: 0.1 }, filters: [{ id: 'nes' }] },\n499|  environment: { pixel: null, filters: [{ id: 'cel', params: { bands: 4 } }] },\n500|});\n(more lines below)","final_result":true,"kind":"output","task_id":"01a1aa23-fe43-78d3-9556-1fea77493095"},"kind":"task_lifecycle","run_stream":{"id":"01a1aa23-76a1-7912-8c1f-0bbd20af817a","kind":"run"},"task_id":"01a1aa23-fe43-78d3-9556-1fea77493095","task_stream":{"id":"01a1aa23-fe43-78d3-9556-1fea77493095","kind":"task"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c5a1","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1aa23-73f9-7152-a6b5-3fcdf5d3c2f1"},"sequence":299,"recorded_at":1780531400000593,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01a1aa23-76a1-7912-8c1f-0bbd20af817a","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"command_id":"01a1aa23-76a1-7912-8c1f-0bbd20af817a","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"01a1aa23-fe43-78d3-9556-1fea77493095"},"kind":"task_lifecycle","run_stream":{"id":"01a1aa23-76a1-7912-8c1f-0bbd20af817a","kind":"run"},"task_id":"01a1aa23-fe43-78d3-9556-1fea77493095","task_stream":{"id":"01a1aa23-fe43-78d3-9556-1fea77493095","kind":"task"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c5a3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1aa23-73f9-7152-a6b5-3fcdf5d3c2f1"},"sequence":300,"recorded_at":1780531400000595,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01a1aa23-76a1-7912-8c1f-0bbd20af817a","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"call_id":"call_01a1aa23f962747a97ab574e7954893f","command_id":"01a1aa23-76a1-7912-8c1f-0bbd20af817a","correlation_facts":{"outcome":"success","tool_name":"read_file"},"kind":"tool_result","run_stream":{"id":"01a1aa23-76a1-7912-8c1f-0bbd20af817a","kind":"run"},"text":"Read text file `Fit Getaway-Wii Fit-muse-wonder-1.0-max-20261104-221652/GUIDE.md`.\n1|# Pixel Engine: the guide\n2|\n3|> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. The kit: `README.md` (what is where), this guide, `API.md`, `pixel-engine.js`.\n4|\n5|This guide is for an AI agent (or a person) building a game on Pixel Engine from the kit:\n6|`pixel-engine.js` plus this guide, `API.md` and a starter game. Read it whole once; it is\n7|written to be enough. `API.md` has every export with its exact signature: search it when you\n8|need a name or a parameter, don't read it whole.\n9|\n10|Contents: 1 Host it · 2 Mental model · 3 Quick start · 4 The API by system · 5 Recipes ·\n11|6 Rules · 7 Common mistakes · 8 Debugging and checking · 9 Before you hand back · 10 Versions\n12|\n13|## 1. Host it\n14|\n15|A game is static files next to the engine. Nothing to install, no build step:\n16|\n17|```\n18|my-game/\n19|  pixel-engine.js     the engine (never edit it; upgrade by replacing it)\n20|  index.html          a page with <div id=\"app\"> that loads game.js as a module\n21|  game.js             your game: import { ... } from './pixel-engine.js'\n22|  assets/             your own .glb models, if any (optional)\n23|```\n24|\n25|The kit's `index.html` and `game.js` are exactly that: copy the kit folder and edit `game.js`.\n26|Split a bigger game into more modules (`import { Level } from './level.js'`) as usual.\n27|\n28|- **Serve it over HTTP.** ES modules don't load from `file://`. Any static server works:\n29|  `npx serve .`, `python3 -m http.server`, GitHub Pages, Netlify, an S3 bucket.\n30|- **One import.** `pixel-engine.js` is one self-contained ES module (about 4.8 MB, 1.6 MB\n31|  gzipped). It holds the engine, three.js r186 (as `THREE` and `TSL`), Rapier physics with its\n32|  wasm, the hero (model, rig and every animation clip), three built-in models (`assets/hero.glb`,\n33|  `assets/coin.glb`, `assets/tree.glb`) and the engine's CSS. Don't load anything else.\n34|- **The page is the game's.** Importing the engine adds its CSS, which makes the page a\n35|  full-screen game: `html, body` don't scroll and `#app` fills the window. To embed a game in a\n36|  bigger page, give the container your own size and position (your CSS comes later and wins).\n37|- **Browsers.** WebGPU only: a recent Chrome or Edge, Safari 26, Firefox 141 on Windows. A\n38|  browser without it is told so (\"Failed to start: WebGPU unavailable: …\"). Phones and tablets\n39|  get on-screen touch controls by themselves.\n40|- **Your own models.** Put GLBs in `assets/` and call `ctx.loadModel('assets/ship.glb')`\n41|  (paths are relative to the page). To ship one file with no `assets/` folder, set\n42|  `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__ = { 'assets/ship.glb': 'data:model/gltf-binary;base64,...' }`\n43|  in a classic `<script>` before the module loads.\n44|\n45|## 2. Mental model\n46|\n47|**The engine renders 3D as pixel art.** You build a small 3D world out of chunky low-poly\n48|shapes and toon materials in a 16-colour palette. The renderer draws it at a low art\n49|resolution (480×270, wider or taller to fit the screen), adds pixel outlines and scales it\n50|up by whole pixels. You never draw sprites; the pixel art comes from the camera and the\n51|pipeline. A **look** can change that: other palettes, CRT, cel shading, or clean\n52|full-resolution rendering, per layer (section 4, *Looks*).\n53|\n54|**The engine owns the machinery; a game is a plain object with hooks.** The engine owns the\n55|renderer, camera rig, physics stepping, the sun and ambient light, input, audio, particles,\n56|the pixel HUD, the loading screen and the debug tools. You write a class:\n57|\n58|| Hook | When | Do here |\n59|| --- | --- | --- |\n60|| `name`, `assets` | before start | the title; models to start downloading early |\n61|| `async setup(ctx)` | once | build the level: meshes, colliders, models, characters, triggers |\n62|| `fixedUpdate(ctx, dt)` | 60 times a second, before each physics step | movement, forces, anything gameplay that must be exact |\n63|| `update(ctx, dt)` | every rendered frame, after physics | animation, visuals, pickups, HUD |\n64|| `cameraTarget(ctx)` | every frame | return the `Vector3` the camera follows |\n65|| `status(ctx)` | tooling | a one-line summary (`'coins 3/4'`), shown by the check tool |\n66|| `fingerprint(ctx)` | tooling | the game's own state as a string (positions, counters), part of `engine.fingerprint()` so a replay can be compared |\n67|| `snapshot(ctx)`, `restore(ctx, state)`, `beforeRestore(ctx)` | save-states | the game's own state as plain data (as it is, not through JSON), and back (`await engine.snapshot()`, `await engine.restore(snap)`); physics bodies are the engine's: keep their handles; `beforeRestore` leaves a mode with its own camera first |\n68|| `onCameraChange(ctx)`, `eyePosition(ctx)`, `dispose(ctx)` | optional | camera swaps, first-person eye, cleanup |\n69|| `ready(ctx)` | optional: when the engine starts running the game, and after each `loadGame` | page handles for tools (`window.__MYGAME__`) |\n70|\n71|`ctx` (the `GameContext`) is the same object in every hook: `ctx.scene` (three.js scene),\n72|`ctx.physics`, `ctx.input`, `ctx.camera`, `ctx.audio`, `ctx.particles`, `ctx.hud`,\n73|`ctx.lights`, `ctx.palette`, `ctx.loadModel`, `ctx.time`, `ctx.random` and `ctx.engine`.\n74|\n75|**Physics owns positions; visuals follow.** Bodies move in fixed 60 Hz steps (Rapier).\n76|Meshes are interpolated between steps, so motion is smooth at any frame rate. A character\n77|controller moves its body in `fixedUpdate`; its model is posed in `update`.\n78|\n79|**Scale.** One unit is one metre, Y is up, the ground is usually at y = 0. The hero is 1.6 m\n80|tall, runs at 6.5 m/s and jumps 1.7 m (double jump 2.4 m, triple 3.5 m; it also grabs ledges).\n81|At the default zoom the camera shows about 13.5 m vertically, so one art pixel is about\n82|5 cm: give shapes at least 0.25 m so they read.\n83|\n84|## 3. Quick start\n85|\n86|A complete game, and the kit's `game.js`. Walk with WASD or the arrows, jump with Space,\n87|collect the four coins.\n88|\n89|```js quickstart\n90|// Coin Garden: walk (WASD / arrows / stick), jump (Space), collect every coin.\n91|import { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n92|\n93|const { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n94|\n95|/** A box you can see (toon material, palette colour) and stand on (a static collider). */\n96|function block(ctx, [x, y, z], [w, h, d], color) {\n97|  ctx.physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\n98|  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\n99|  mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n100|  mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\n101|  return mesh;\n102|}\n103|\n104|class CoinGarden {\n105|  name = 'Coin Garden';\n106|  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb']; // start downloading before setup runs\n107|  coins = [];\n108|  collected = 0;\n109|  won = false;\n110|  target = new Vector3();\n111|  ground = new Vector3();\n112|\n113|  async setup(ctx) {\n114|    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n115|    // Level: static geometry merged into one draw call per material; one collider per block.\n116|    scene.add(\n117|      ...mergeStaticMeshes([\n118|        block(ctx, [0, -0.5, 0], [24, 1, 24], palette.green),\n119|        block(ctx, [4, 0.5, -2], [3, 1, 3], palette.slate),\n120|        block(ctx, [7, 1.25, -2], [3, 2.5, 3], palette.teal),\n121|        block(ctx, [-5, 0.75, 4], [2, 1.5, 2], palette.orange),\n122|      ]),\n123|    );\n124|    // Coins: a model, spinning; a trigger volume collects it when the hero walks in.\n125|    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb', { castShadow: false });\n126|    for (const [x, y, z] of [[4, 1.4, -2], [7, 2.9, -2], [-5, 1.9, 4], [-2, 0.4, -5]]) {\n127|      const mesh = coin.scene.clone(true);\n128|      mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n129|      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors'); // characters & objects can get their own look\n130|      scene.add(mesh);\n131|      const item = { mesh, taken: false };\n132|      physics.trigger({ cylinder: { halfHeight: 0.6, radius: 0.5 } }, [x, y, z], { tag: 'character', once: true, onEnter: () => this.collect(ctx, item) });\n133|      this.coins.push(item);\n134|    }\n135|    // The hero: the bundled model and clips on a Mario-64-style controller.\n136|    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false });\n137|    this.model = hero.scene;\n138|    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n139|    this.shadow = new ContactShadow(0.42);\n140|    scene.add(this.model, this.shadow);\n141|    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 4], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n142|    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n143|  }\n144|\n145|  collect(ctx, item) {\n146|    item.taken = true;\n147|    item.mesh.visible = false;\n148|    this.collected++;\n149|    ctx.audio.play('coin');\n150|    ctx.particles.burst('sparkle', item.mesh.position);\n151|  }\n152|\n153|  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n154|    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input); // filled in place after the first step\n155|    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input); // movement and forces: fixed 60 Hz step\n156|  }\n157|\n158|  update(ctx, dt) {\n159|    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha); // pose and interpolate\n160|    const feet = this.model.position;\n161|    const ground = ctx.physics.groundBelow(this.ground.set(feet.x, feet.y + 0.1, feet.z), 20, this.hero.body);\n162|    if (ground) this.shadow.place(feet.x, ground.y, feet.z, feet.y - ground.y);\n163|    for (const c of this.coins) if (!c.taken) c.mesh.rotation.y += dt * 3;\n164|    if (!this.won && this.collected === this.coins.length) {\n165|      this.won = true;\n166|      this.hero.celebrate();\n167|      ctx.audio.play('fanfare');\n168|    }\n169|    ctx.hud.clear();\n170|    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `COINS ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`, { color: 'sand' });\n171|    if (this.won) ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'YOU WIN!', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2, shadow: 'navy' });\n172|  }\n173|\n174|  cameraTarget() {\n175|    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 4);\n176|    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n177|  }\n178|\n179|  onCameraChange(ctx) {\n180|    this.hero.setLockDepth(ctx.camera.lockDepth); // side view: stay on the lane\n181|    this.model.visible = !ctx.camera.hidesTarget; // first person: no body in the way\n182|  }\n183|\n184|  status() {\n185|    return `coins ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`;\n186|  }\n187|}\n188|\n189|/** A fresh game: the page starts one, and `node check.mjs --headless` plays one in Node (section 8). */\n190|export const createGame = () => new CoinGarden();\n191|\n192|if (typeof document !== 'undefined') await Engine.start(createGame(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'iso' } }));\n193|```\n194|\n195|To make it yours: change the blocks (the level), the coin spots, the camera preset, the HUD,\n196|the win condition. `withUrlOptions(options)` adds the review flags from the page URL on top of\n197|your options (`?camera=side`, `?zoom=1.5`, `?look=noir`, `?debug=1`; section 8): the camera is\n198|merged key by key, and with no flags your options are used as they are.\n199|\n200|## 4. The API by system\n201|\n202|Everything below comes from `./pixel-engine.js`. `API.md` has the full signatures.\n203|\n204|### Starting\n205|\n206|```js\n207|const engine = await Engine.start(new MyGame(), withUrlOptions({  // + review flags from the URL\n208|  container: document.getElementById('app'),\n209|  camera: { preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.2 },  // one preset per game (see Camera)\n210|  look: LOOK_PRESETS.pixel_heroes,       // optional: the art style (see Looks)\n211|  background: PALETTE.sky,               // clear colour (default PALETTE.night)\n212|}));\n213|await engine.loadGame(new Level2());     // next level: same renderer, camera, input, audio\n214|```\n215|\n216|Shipping options: the debug panel and the engine hotkeys (P, R, \\`, [, ], M) are off outside\n217|`?debug=1`. Players get those options in the settings panel instead (look, pixel art, pixel size,\n218|quality, frame rate, sound, debug info; saved per game): the ⚙ button opens it, top right\n219|(`settings: { button: 'top-left' }` moves it; `false` hides it, for a game that opens it from its\n220|own menu with `engine.settings.open()`). A game that uses R, P or M itself moves that hotkey for\n221|`?debug=1` with `debugKeys: { resolution: 'F2' }`. `touchButtons: [{ label: 'A', code: 'Space', hint: 'jump' }]`\n222|replaces the on-screen buttons.\n223|`loadGame` can be called from anywhere (a trigger, `update`); the swap happens after the\n224|frame. It unloads the old game: scene objects, physics, triggers, particles, HUD, music and\n225|lights are freed, so a level never leaks into the next.\n226|\n227|### Building a level\n228|\n229|- **Materials:** `toonMaterial(color)` is a cached 3-band toon material. Use palette\n230|  colours: `ctx.palette.green` or `PALETTE.green`. The 16 names: `ink plum red orange sand\n231|  lime green teal navy blue sky cyan white mist slate night`. Options: `{ map, vertexColors, rim, dissolve }`.\n232|- **Meshes:** plain three.js (`new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(c))`).\n233|  Set `castShadow` / `receiveShadow`. For the static level, build meshes and add\n234|  `...mergeStaticMeshes(meshes)`: one draw call per material instead of one per block (don't\n235|  add the sources too). Keep anything that moves separate.\n236|- **Colliders:** `physics.addStaticBox({ position, halfExtents, rotationY?, friction? })` and\n237|  `physics.addStaticCylinder(position, halfHeight, radius)`. One per solid block, matching\n238|  the mesh (half extents are half the size). Anything else through `RAPIER` and\n239|  `physics.world` (see the top-down recipe's crates).\n240|- **Collider tags** the hero understands: `physics.tag(collider, 'climbable')`, `'pushable'`,\n241|  `'grabbable'`, `'slippery'`, `'noLedge'`, `'noCamera'` (camera rays ignore it).\n242|- **Models:** `const m = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb')` returns `{ scene, animations }`,\n243|  a fresh clone each call, materials converted to toon. `m.scene.clone(true)` copies a static model.\n244|  List models in the game's `assets` so they download while the engine starts.\n245|- **Look layers:** `setLookLayer(object, 'actors')` puts characters and objects on the actors\n246|  layer (children follow); everything else is environment. Looks can treat them differently.\n247|- **Shadow blob:** `new ContactShadow(radius)`, placed every frame with\n248|  `shadow.place(x, groundY, z, heightAboveGround)` (see the quick start).\n249|\n250|**Models and textures as data.** Besides GLBs, the kit has a library of models (crates, stalls,\n251|lamps, trees, doors, weapons...) and pixel-art textures, and a format to write your own as plain\n252|data: `buildModel('market-stall', { palette: { cloth: 'blue' } })` gives merged toon meshes, their\n253|collider data and light, and `textureMap('cobbles')` a map for `toonMaterial`. `ASSETS.md` is their\n254|guide (the format, the loop, the style rules, an example scene), `LIBRARY.md` lists the library,\n255|and `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws what you write. Read `ASSETS.md` before you write one.\n256|\n257|### The hero\n258|\n259|`PlatformerCharacter` is a Mario-64-style controller with the full moveset and animation;\n260|`HERO_MODEL`, `HERO_RIG` and `HERO_CLIPS` are its model and clips:\n261|\n262|```js\n263|const model = (await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false })).scene;\n264|setLookLayer(model, 'actors');\n265|ctx.scene.add(model);\n266|const hero = new PlatformerCharacter(ctx.physics, { position: [0, 0, 0], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n267|hero.attachModel(model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, model), HERO_RIG);\n268|// fixedUpdate: input = readMoveInput(ctx, hero, input); hero.fixedUpdate(dt, input);  (input kept, filled in place)\n269|// update:      hero.updateVisual(model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n270|```\n271|\n272|Controls (`readMoveInput`, camera-relative in every preset): WASD / arrows move (Shift\n273|walks), Space jumps (again on landing: double, triple; wall kicks), C crouches (+ Space\n274|backflip; running: slide, long jump; in the air: ground pound), J punch / kick / dive, F grab\n275|and pull, Z prone, X lie down, V wave, B sit. Ledges, climbing (`climbable`), pushing\n276|(`pushable`), slopes and stairs just work. Gamepads and touch map onto the same keys.\n277|\n278|The game drives it with: `hero.teleport([x, y, z])` (feet position; resets momentum),\n279|`hero.hurt(from)` (knocked back away from `from`, the direction from the hero to what hit it:\n280|enemy position minus hero position; false while `hero.invulnerable > 0`),\n281|`hero.jump('Jump')` (bounce, e.g. off an enemy), `hero.celebrate()`, `hero.lookAt = vector`\n282|(turns the head), `hero.setLockDepth(on)`. It reports `hero.state` (`'idle'`, `'run'`,\n283|`'jump'`, ...), `hero.grounded`, `hero.vy`, `hero.speed`, `hero.facing`, `hero.stats`\n284|(jumps, landings, hurts, ...), `hero.feetInto(v)` and `hero.body` (its Rapier body). Its\n285|collider carries the `'character'` tag. For a simpler walk-and-jump capsule there is\n286|`CharacterController`.\n287|\n288|### Input\n289|\n290|`ctx.input.isDown('KeyE')` (held), `wasPressed('KeyE')` (once, in `update`),\n291|`consumePress('KeyE')` (once, in `fixedUpdate`: it keeps a press for 150 ms so none is lost\n292|between steps), `moveAxis()` (`{ x, y }` from keys, stick or touch). Codes are\n293|`KeyboardEvent.code` values: `KeyA`, `Space`, `ArrowUp`, `Digit1`, `ShiftLeft`.\n294|\n295|### Physics queries and triggers\n296|\n297|```js\n298|const t = ctx.physics.trigger({ box: [1, 1.5, 0.5] }, [x, y, z], {\n299|  tag: 'character', once: true,                // only the hero; remove itself after the first enter\n300|  onEnter: () => win(), onExit: () => {},\n301|});                                            // shapes: box (half extents), sphere, capsule, cylinder\n302|t.position.set(4, 1, 0); t.enabled = false; t.remove();\n303|ctx.physics.groundBelow(origin, 20, hero.body); // { y, distance } | null\n304|ctx.physics.castRay(from, dir, maxDistance);    // { distance, collider, normal, point } | null\n305|ctx.physics.remove(colliderOrBody);             // e.g. open a gate\n306|```\n307|\n308|Triggers see dynamic and kinematic bodies (the hero, crates), not level geometry. Dynamic\n309|bodies: `RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()` and `RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(...)` on\n310|`ctx.physics.world`, then `ctx.physics.bind(body, mesh)` so the mesh follows it.\n311|\n312|### Physics toys: platforms, fields, joints, destruction, cloth\n313|\n314|```js\n315|const lift = ctx.physics.world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.kinematicPositionBased());\n316|ctx.physics.world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(1.2, 0.15, 1.2), lift);\n317|ctx.physics.addMover(lift, { path: [[0, 0.15, 0], [0, 4, 0]], speed: 1.5, hold: 1 }); // the hero rides it\n318|ctx.physics.addMover(disc, { spin: [0, 0.6, 0] });           // also: swing, curve: orbit(...) / pendulum(...)\n319|ctx.physics.conveyor(beltCollider, [2, 0, 0]);               // carries crates and the hero\n320|ctx.physics.fields.add({ box: [1, 4, 1], at: [4, 4, 0], force: [0, 90, 0], falloff: true, drag: 0.5 }); // an updraft\n321|ctx.physics.explode([0, 0.5, 0], { radius: 4, impulse: 12 });\n322|hero.launch(16);                                             // a trampoline\n323|hero.shove = 1.5; hero.weight = 1;                           // push loose bodies; press dynamic floors (both 0 by default)\n324|ropeBridge(ctx, [-3, 0, 0], [3, 0, 0], { planks: 10 });      // also chain, hingeDoor, springPad, seesaw\n325|const breaks = new Breakables(ctx);                          // breaks.add / break / crumble; update(dt, hero.groundCollider)\n326|const flag = clothGrid({ width: 2, height: 1.2, cols: 12, rows: 8, origin: [0, 3, 0], pin: 'left' });\n327|ctx.scene.add(new SoftMesh(flag, toonMaterial(PALETTE.red)));  // flag.step(dt) per fixed step, mesh.sync() per frame\n328|const balls = new InstancedBodies(ctx.physics, ctx.scene, { shape: 'ball', size: 0.2, capacity: 500 }); // one draw call\n329|```\n330|\n331|Movers are functions of their own clock (same at any frame rate). `hero.stats.crushes` counts a\n332|platform coming down on the hero: decide what it does. Ledges are fixed bodies only.\n333|\n334|### Water, weather, grass, trails and decals\n335|\n336|```js\n337|const water = new WaterSurface({ size: [14, 8], at: [0, -0.2, 0], waves: WAVES_CALM, ripples: new RippleField({ size: [14, 8], cells: [112, 64] }) });\n338|const floaters = new Floaters(ctx.physics.world, (x, z) => (water.covers(x, z) ? water.heightAt(x, z) : null));\n339|applySky(ctx.engine, skyAt(19));                              // time of day: sun, colours, background\n340|const rain = new Precipitation({ kind: 'rain', count: 3000 }); // also 'snow'; rain.intensity, rain.wind\n341|const grass = new GrassField({ area: [20, 14], at: [0, 0, 0], count: 6000 });  // grass.push(0, heroFeet, 0.8)\n342|const decals = new Decals(ctx.scene);                          // decals.add('scorch', at, normal, { size: 2 })\n343|ctx.scene.add(water, rain, grass, new Trail({ width: 0.3 }));  // trail.push(p, t); trail.update(t, camera)\n344|```\n345|\n346|Per frame: `water.update(t)`, `rain.update(dt, focus)`, `grass.update(t)`, `decals.update(dt)`;\n347|per fixed step: `floaters.step(dt)` and the ripple field's `step(dt)`.\n348|\n349|### Procedural motion and sprites\n350|\n351|```js\n352|const tail = new SpringChain({ segments: 6, length: 0.12 });   // tail.update(dt, root, [right, up, forward]); tail.points\n353|const squash = new Squash();                                   // squash.kick(-5); squash.update(dt); squash.scale() → [xz, y]\n354|const legs = new LegStepper({ legs: [{ rest: [-0.6, 0.5], partners: [1] }, { rest: [0.6, 0.5], partners: [0] }] });\n355|legs.update(dt, body, yaw, velocity, (x, z) => 0);             // legs.feet[i].at; knee = twoBoneIK(hip, foot, a, b, pole)\n356|const sheet = drawSheet({ frame: [16, 16], frames: 4 }, (g, f) => { g.fillStyle = '#ffcd75'; g.fillRect(4, 4 + (f % 2), 8, 8); });\n357|const sprites = new SpriteBatch(sheet, { capacity: 100 });     // ctx.scene.add(sprites); sprites.spawn(at, { size: 1 })\n358|```\n359|\n360|Sprites face the camera, keep square pixels and are one draw per sheet: `sprites.set(i, { at, frame, flip })`.\n361|\n362|A ragdoll from the hero model: `const doll = new Ragdoll(ctx.physics, model, HERO_RAGDOLL); doll.enable({ velocity })`,\n363|then `doll.sync()` every frame instead of the mixer; `doll.release({ ground: 0 })` hands it back (blend into `GetUp`).\n364|\n365|### Guards, paths, flocks, cars and bullets\n366|\n367|```js\n368|const nav = NavGrid.fromRows(['#######', '#..c..#', '#.....#', '#######'], { blocked: '#c' });\n369|const path = nav.path([-2, 0, -0.5], [2, 0, 0.5]);         // A* (corners only); nav.lineOfSight(ax, az, bx, bz)\n370|const flock = new Boids(80, { bounds: { min: [-8, 2, -8], max: [8, 5, 8] } }); // flock.step(dt); flock.pos\n371|const car = new Vehicle(ctx.physics, { at: [0, 1, 0] });   // car.drive({ throttle, steer, handbrake }, dt) per fixed step\n372|const pool = new BulletPool(3000);                         // emitter({ pattern: 'spiral', every: 0.06 }).update(pool, dt, at, aim)\n373|```\n374|\n375|### Mouse picking and rewind\n376|\n377|```js\n378|ctx.camera.rayAt(ctx.engine.input.pointer.x, ctx.engine.input.pointer.y, origin, dir); // then ctx.physics.castRay(origin, dir, 1000)\n379|const rewind = new Rewind(ctx.physics, { seconds: 5 }); // rewind.trackAll(); per step: rewind.rewinding = input.isDown('KeyR')\n380|```\n381|\n382|### Procedural generation\n383|\n384|```js\n385|const noise = createNoise(7); // noise.fbm2(x * 0.05, z * 0.05) about -1..1; noise.ridged2 for crests\n386|const land = new Terrain({ size: [40, 30] }).generate((x, z) => 4 * noise.fbm2(x * 0.05, z * 0.05)); // land.erode(); land.attach(ctx.physics); ctx.scene.add(land.mesh)\n387|const wfc = new Wfc(patternTiles([{ pattern: ['#.#', '#.#', '#.#'] }, { pattern: ['###', '###', '###'] }]), 10, 8, { seed: 3 }); // wfc.run(); wfc.result\n388|const plant = turtle(expand(PLANTS.fern.system, 5), PLANTS.fern.turtle); // plant.branches: { from, to, radius }, plant.leaves\n389|```\n390|\n391|### GPU compute, monitors and mirrors\n392|\n393|```js\n394|const swarm = new GpuSwarm({ count: 32768 }); ctx.scene.add(swarm.mesh); // per frame: swarm.update(ctx.engine.renderer, dt)\n395|const cam = new RenderView({ size: [160, 90] }); const stopCam = cam.attach(ctx.engine.renderer, ctx.scene); // screen.material = cam.screenMaterial(); RenderView.screen(screen)\n396|const mirror = new Mirror({ size: [6, 3] }); ctx.scene.add(mirror.mesh); const stopMirror = mirror.attach(ctx.engine.renderer, ctx.scene);\n397|// in dispose(): stopCam(); stopMirror(); cam.dispose(); mirror.dispose(); swarm.dispose() (a level unload also drops the listeners)\n398|const holo = hologramMaterial({ color: PALETTE.cyan }); // forceFieldMaterial, lavaMaterial, marbleMaterial, woodMaterial, crystalMaterial\n399|```\n400|\n401|### Behaviour trees, cutscenes, sound in space\n402|\n403|```js\n404|const tree = new BehaviorTree(bt.selector('npc', bt.sequence('flee', bt.condition('near?', (b) => b.near), bt.action('run', run)), bt.action('wander', wander)), board); // tree.tick(dt) per step\n405|const scene = new Timeline({ shots: [{ at: 0, position: [0, 6, 10], target: [0, 1, 0] }, { at: 4, position: [-1, 2, 4], target: [0, 1, 0] }], cues: [{ at: 1, run: openDoor }], lines: [{ at: 0.5, until: 3, text: 'HELLO.' }] }); // scene.play(); scene.update(dt); scene.camera(); scene.skip()\n406|const hum = ctx.audio.loop('coin', { volume: 0 }); // per frame: const s = spatialize({ position: ears, right }, at, { max: 20 }); hum.set({ volume: s.gain, pan: s.pan, muffle: blocked ? 0.85 : 0 })\n407|```\n408|\n409|### Camera\n410|\n411|| Preset | View | Notes |\n412|| --- | --- | --- |\n413|| `iso` | orthographic 3/4 view, 32° pitch, 45° yaw | the default; `pitch`, `yaw` configurable |\n414|| `topdown` | straight down, screen up = −Z | |\n415|| `side` | straight side-on, screen right = +X | `ctx.camera.lockDepth` is true: pass it to the hero |\n416|| `third` | perspective orbit behind the player | drag or Q/E to orbit |\n417|| `first` | first person, mouse look (click locks the pointer) | `ctx.camera.hidesTarget`: hide your model |\n418|| `free`, `fixed` | authoring fly-cam; a frozen view | `{ preset: 'fixed', position, target, fov }` |\n419|\n420|Config: `{ preset, zoom, minZoom, maxZoom, viewHeight, pitch, yaw, fov, distance, stiffness }`.\n421|Players zoom with the wheel or + / -. `engine.setCamera(config, { syncUrl: false })` swaps\n422|the preset during play (a cutscene, a minigame); the game's `onCameraChange` fixes up\n423|anything preset-specific.\n424|\n425|### HUD\n426|\n427|A pixel canvas over the game, in art pixels, drawn after the filters (always crisp).\n428|It is retained: `clear()` then redraw each frame.\n429|\n430|```js\n431|ctx.hud.clear();\n432|ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `SCORE ${score}`, { color: 'sand' });            // 5×7 font, UPPERCASE\n433|ctx.hud.text(6, 6, 'LIVES 3', { anchor: 'top-right' });              // anchors: top-left (default), top,\n434|ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });        //   top-right, left, center, right, bottom-*\n435|ctx.hud.rect(0, 0, ctx.hud.width, 12, 'ink', 'bottom-left');\n436|ctx.hud.sprite(6, 20, ['.oo.', 'oyyo', '.oo.'], { o: 'orange', y: 'sand' }); // '.' is transparent\n437|```\n438|\n439|### Audio\n440|\n441|```js\n442|ctx.audio.play('coin');                       // built-in: jump doubleJump land coin step skid punch groundPound whoosh hurt fanfare\n443|ctx.audio.play('coin', { pitch: 5, volume: 0.6, pan: -0.3 });\n444|ctx.audio.register('zap', { wave: 'square', duty: 0.25, freq: 1200, freqEnd: 200, decay: 0.15, volume: 0.3 });\n445|ctx.audio.playMusic(SONG);                    // a Song (data, see the top-down recipe); playMusic(null) stops\n446|await ctx.audio.load('door', 'assets/door.ogg');\n447|```\n448|\n449|Sounds are synthesised from data, so a game needs no audio files. Browsers start sound on\n450|the first key or tap; before that, calls are silent (and still counted in `ctx.audio.counts`).\n451|\n452|### Particles and lights\n453|\n454|```js\n455|ctx.particles.burst('dust', at);              // built-in: dust skid sparkle smoke impact\n456|ctx.particles.burst('sparkle', at, { count: 20, colors: ['white', 'cyan'] });\n457|ctx.particles.register('ember', { count: [3, 5], life: [0.6, 1], speed: [1, 2], gravity: -2, size: [1, 1], colors: ['sand', 'orange', 'red'] });\n458|\n459|const torch = ctx.lights.request({ position: [3, 1.6, 4], color: 0xffa040, intensity: 6, radius: 7, flicker: 'torch' });\n460|ctx.lights.request({ follow: heroModel, offset: [0, 1.4, 0], intensity: 5, radius: 6, priority: 8 });\n461|torch.release();\n462|ctx.engine.sun.intensity = 0.6;               // the sun and ambient light are the engine's: dim them for night\n463|```\n464|\n465|Particle sizes are whole art pixels and colours are palette names. Point lights come from a\n466|fixed pool (4 to 16 by quality), handed to the most important requests: never add your own\n467|`PointLight`s (each one recompiles every material).\n468|\n469|### Game feel and scene changes\n470|\n471|```js\n472|ctx.engine.hitstop(0.08);                    // freeze the game 0.08 s when a hit lands (rendering goes on)\n473|ctx.engine.shake.add(0.4);                   // screen shake: trauma 0..1 that drains; whole art pixels\n474|ctx.engine.timeScale = 0.25;                 // slow motion (1 normal, 0 a frozen menu); loadGame resets it\n475|ctx.engine.screen.flash('white', { duration: 0.1 });\n476|ctx.engine.screen.shockwave(blastAt, { radius: 0.4 });        // a ring that pushes the picture outward\n477|await ctx.engine.screen.cover('iris', { center: heroAt });    // fade iris diamonds dissolve dither blinds wipe curtain mosaic\n478|await ctx.engine.loadGame(new Level2());\n479|await ctx.engine.screen.reveal('iris');\n480|ctx.tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' }); // game time, cleared on unload\n481|ctx.tweens.call(1.5, () => spawnWave());     // a timer\n482|ctx.engine.setSunDirection([1, 0.3, 0.2]);   // a low dawn sun; the next level resets it\n483|```\n484|\n485|Transitions, flashes and shockwaves are uniforms at the end of the pipeline: they never\n486|compile a shader, work with every look, and keep running while a level loads.\n487|\n488|### Looks\n489|\n490|A `Look` sets the art style per layer: `{ scene, actors, environment }`. Each layer is\n491|`{ pixel: { size, outline, crease } | null, filters: [...] }` (`null` renders it clean, at\n492|full resolution); `scene` filters apply to the whole frame on top.\n493|\n494|```js\n495|engine.setLook(LOOK_PRESETS.noir);            // or Engine.start(game, { look }) / ?look=noir\n496|engine.setLook({\n497|  scene: [{ id: 'vignette' }],\n498|  actors: { pixel: { size: 2, outline: 0.8, crease: 0.1 }, filters: [{ id: 'nes' }] },\n499|  environment: { pixel: null, filters: [{ id: 'cel', params: { bands: 4 } }] },\n500|});\n(more lines below)"}}
